/*\
\
Common partitioning mistakes\
\
1. Constraint exclusion is not enabled\
	if not enabled, it will cause to always including every partition for all queries. Long queries...\
\
2. Missing indexes on partition\
	-- try to replicate the same indexes that are there on master/parent table to all partitions too.\
	-- Make sure all the parent constraints are also added to partition tables\
\
3. Missing or mismatch security permission on partittion tables\
	-- Forgetting to assign the same permissions to each table as the parent\
\
4. Queries not using partition field at all\
\
	-- big mistake\
	-- always make sure WHERE clause needs to filter on constants\
	-- For parametrized query , they will therefore not work right at all;\
		and in case of functions say those that change their value , like say current date ,\
		will also optimize poorly\
5. In general, keep the  WHERE clause as simple as possible, to improve the odds that the \
	optimizer will construct the exclusion proof you are looking for \
\
6. Query overhead for partitioning is proportional to the number of partitions. It is  barely\
	noticable with only 10 or 20 partitions, but if you have 100s you can expect it to be \
	significant impact on your database\
\
*/}